the day before my bar mitzvah I was  sitting in my rabbis office and I  started to cry it's not because I was  just afraid of the big day it's not  because I didn't know which girl I was  going to ask for the first dance at my  disco themed bar mitzvah party the next  night which was awesome because I had a  secret and tell if you understand a  little bit about what that felt like  think about the person you're sitting  next to either you're right or left  really look at them and imagine asking  them are you male or female you might  not need to ask that imagine asking  where are you from you know what's your  nationality what's your background now  ask that person do you believe in God  that's probably going to be a little  harder for you right and even as I am  talking about it here I'm afraid to talk  to you about this because I'm an atheist  and as I say that to you you form  probably some assumptions about what  that means but think about what I just  told you I told you what I don't believe  in not anything about what I do believe  in or what I do care about as a problem  with the word atheism but it's a bigger  problem than just the GSB or me feeling  awkward in front of you guys in the u.s.  there are seven states where atheists  are barred from holding a public office  by constitutional law in Arkansas  atheists are barred from being a witness  to a trial those are old laws and  impossible to enforce I hope but the  court of public opinion is no better  unfortunately about half of americans  say they would never vote for an atheist  for public office regardless of how well  qualified they work well that means to  me is like half of you guys wouldn't  vote for me because of my religious  preference or non religious preference  but  that's prejudice right and this climate  it it makes non-religious people in the  u.s. feel less American or unamerican  but fifteen percent of americans are  non-religious and that's not even  including the people who define  themselves as religious but also say  they don't believe in God it's like 45  million people in fact the  fastest-growing religious preference in  the u.s. is non-religious it's the only  one that's grown in all 50 states in the  past generation and what prejudices is  bad weather it seems a big group or a  small group this is my story and  religious prejudice is like a much  bigger issue than I can tackle but I can  talk to you about atheism because it's  what I've known sorry so this happens  because of a mistrust and I'm willing to  bet that those of you who are in the  room who are non-religious have felt  that mistrust in some point in your life  it's natural to assume if you drive your  your morals from God or from Scripture  that atheists have no morals but this is  not true so it's a big problem and to  help proposed like a solution I want to  take you back to my rabbis office so  there I am sitting across from him and  as I'm reading the Torah it's the scroll  I'm reading Hebrew which I knew for like  a month and then forgot and and I start  to cry and I say I I can't do this oh  you know I'm about to read this in front  of my family and my friends and I don't  believe it I don't believe in God and he  leans back he strokes his beard and and  he asked me two questions he said first  what did you learn from your Torah  portion which was the story of Abraham  Isaac Abraham and Isaac and then he  asked me if in 10 years you're driving  on Lakeshore Drive and you see a member  of your Hebrew school class on the side  of the road with a flat tire what will  you do  and I said oh I'd stop and change their  tire what he didn't know as I had just  learned to change a tire which to me was  like so exciting and like really my  passage into manhood like forget the bar  mitzvah stuff and and so he stood up out  of his chair and he reached his hand out  and he said Evan on behalf of the Jewish  community I welcome you and even though  and that was great right it made it made  me feel much better and I went through  that with the bar mitzvah but I want to  point out what he did there he didn't  take my lack of belief as an answer in  itself he pushed me he asked me what I  do believe in and he made sure that the  values i held matched up with those of  the community that he led and what it  did for me was i realized i can decide  this for myself i need to think about  what my beliefs really are I'm a  humanist a humanist takes to work a look  at the world around and sees that  innocent people are sometimes punished  the world is unjust and therefore there  is no competent moral management a  humanist says so you look at this  sometimes lousy world and think it's  then up to me I'm a superintendent of my  own life I it's up to me to solve human  problems even though I know I can never  fully complete that task what it means  to me in short is like I don't believe  in life after death so I believe that  this life is the one that matters and  the reason I say that specifically is  there's a misunderstanding about those  who don't believe in Heaven and Hell or  a judgment from on high and I want to  read you a quick quote from a pastor who  I actually quite respect there  represents or the other point of view  which is that if your time on earth  where all there is to this life I would  suggest you start living it up  immediately you could forget about being  good and ethical and you wouldn't have  to worry about any consequences of your  actions you can indulge yourself in  total self-centeredness because your  actions would have no long-term  consequences to point out what's wrong  with that and a pic on one of you give  do you believe in God if you decided  tomorrow for yourself that God didn't  exist and that there was no one judging  you would you go on a spree of raping  and pillaging I thought not but it's  like it's good to confirm what this mrs.  is that we evolved to help each other  and there's nothing more important to my  long-term happiness than the effect I  have on the people around me on you  unfortunately I you know I realized that  this this prejudice comes from a place  of just not knowing right when we don't  know something we fear it fear leads to  prejudice and the non-religious don't do  a great job of representing what we  stand for what we believe in because  it's this amorphous group of all kinds  of people I'm just one of them I don't  speak for the group but I felt this  prejudice when i was when i was 10 i  went to my friend andy's house a lot on  the weekends to play with legos he had  this like current-day lego set with a  train and I had the futuristic one with  the monorail and this Saturday I stayed  over and his family who are Catholic  took me to church which is really nice  and then after church we had waffles and  his mom had never talked to a Jewish  person before so she started asking me  questions about my religion which was  really cool but I had no idea I was  speaking behalf on all Jewish people of  all Jewish people right then sorry guys  she asked me as we're eating waffles do  you believe in God and I said no and she  said but surely you must believe in some  higher power and I'm like pouring serum  no and then and she said but heaven you  must have such a big head to not believe  that there's someone watching over you  someone someone guiding you you must be  so arrogant and that sounds pretty rough  but at the time I really was totally  focused on making sure there was maple  syrup in every little square of that  eggo waffle  so I wasn't upset by it but it's still  stuck with me and actually what I want  to show you is what I wish I could have  shown her at that time that sort of  represents the way I think about the  world there it is does anyone know what  this photo is it's really hard to see a  little bit closer no that's earth that  little dot this is this is a photo taken  by the Voyager spacecraft as it left our  solar system NASA had it turned around  one last time before was out of range  and take this photo so it's actually the  furthest away photo of Earth we have  earth is is that is the little dot  within the circle it's one pixel one  pale blue pixel and I look at this and I  think to myself I try to convince myself  that this pale blue dot and all that's  around it was created for one of the 10  million or so species of life on that  pale blue dot we're further that it was  created for one ethnic or religious  subdivision of that species I can't do  it for me this is another lesson that  science which I believe is the best way  we have to understand the world around  us is just the lesson after lesson in  humility we do not live in a privileged  reference frame our preferences don't  count therefore you are the custodian of  your own life's meeting so the next time  you meet someone who's an atheist  instead of starting from this blank  slate on which you might assume things  start from this I don't speak for other  people but it's a place to start and  more importantly when you meet someone  whose values differ from yours ask them  about it talk about it try to bridge  that gap and understand each other I  mean it's so important like my view of  the world impacts everything I do every  day but we don't talk about it so I want  to encourage you to think about that  person next to you again and actually  ask them what do you believe in thanks  you
 

